    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've noticed that sometimes certain notes or certain parts of the frequency spectrum feel almost... unpredictable or \"ghostly,\" like they aren't quite part of the main tone. Is there a mathematical reason why certain frequencies might feel like they're lurking just outside the main harmonic structure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That phenomenon is often attributed to the presence of harmonics that are faintly inharmonic, meaning they don't fit perfectly into the main harmonic series. These inharmonic harmonics arise from the flute's distribution of nodes and antinodes, which can cause partial resonance in regions away from the main harmonic series. This partial resonance can produce a soft, \"ghostly\" feel, as if the notes are hovering just outside the main tone. Mathematically, these inharmonic harmonics can be described using the combination of sines and cosines that form the Fourier series.",
      "source": "target",
